The chemistry points first toward copper tungstate, with unreacted CuO/WO₃ or polymorphic WO₃ as plausible secondary phases, but I won’t assume that assignment. I’m now extracting the peak positions and checking reference structures against the full pattern before deciding how many phases the data support.

The peak evidence now supports two phases, not the nominal divalent tungstate CuWO₄: monoclinic WO₃ explains the diagnostic 23.2/23.65/24.46° triplet, while cubic Cu₃WO₆ explains the otherwise unaccounted peaks at 15.76, 20.37, 33.08, 34.31, 58.19, 63.88, 70.80, and 87.65°. CuWO₄ would require strong peaks near 29.6–32.0° that are not present at the expected relative intensity. I’m moving these two candidates into quantitative refinement and will test whether any residual peaks justify a third phase.

The first quantitative pass converged to roughly 75 wt% WO₃ / 25 wt% Cu₃WO₆ before profile optimization. The lattice refinements move both references slightly smaller, exactly as implied by the systematic high-angle peak shifts. I’m continuing with line-shape refinement now; the current fit is still too crude to treat those fractions as final.

A decisive source of the remaining misfit is now clear: the WO₃ 002 reflection at 23.21° is about three times stronger than an unoriented powder model, while its 020/200 neighbors are not. The same orientation-sensitive mismatch recurs at higher-order WO₃ peaks. I’m adding a physically appropriate [001] March–Dollase texture correction for WO₃, then I’ll reassess residual peaks for any true third phase.

After texture correction, the residuals reveal a genuine third phase rather than random unmatched peaks: the series at about 35.68, 38.90, 48.99, 53.55, 66.77, and 89.65° tracks monoclinic CuO with the expected angle-dependent shift. This is also chemically plausible as unreacted precursor. I’m adding CuO at low abundance and testing whether its full reflection series—not just the two strongest lines—improves the refinement.

The three-phase model is now strong (Rwp ≈ 5.6% with a stable, non-singular common profile), but monoclinic and triclinic WO₃ can look deceptively similar. Before finalizing, I’m running matched refinements for the triclinic and orthorhombic alternatives with the same Cu₃WO₆/CuO phases and texture treatment. This will make the WO₃ polymorph assignment evidence-based rather than just temperature-based.

The matched polymorph test favors triclinic WO₃ strongly: Rwp falls from 5.57% (monoclinic P2₁/n) to 4.51% (triclinic P‑1), while orthorhombic Pnma is decisively poor at 17.49%. The refined triclinic distortion is subtle—α ≈ 89.998°, γ ≈ 89.977°—so I’m treating this as a near-monoclinic low-symmetry WO₃ assignment and doing repeat refinements plus residual checks before locking it in.

The final constrained triclinic model is now numerically stable: Rwp = 4.480%, GOF = 1.862, no SVD singularities, and final maximum shift/s.u. = 0.38. The crystalline fractions are 59.70 wt% WO₃, 33.40 wt% Cu₃WO₆, and 6.91 wt% CuO. I’m exporting all deliverables directly from this exact project state and then reopening them for consistency checks.

